/*!
    \page guide-building.html
    \title Building and Running
    \nextpage {Architecture}

    \section1 Requirements

    \list
        \li Felgo 4 SDK with Qt 6.8.3 and Qt Creator.
        \li CMake 3.16 or newer and Ninja (both come with the SDK).
        \li A C++20 compiler. On Windows the SDK ships MinGW 13.
        \li Optional: \c ccache and \c lld are used automatically when they are installed.
        \li Optional: Python 3 with Pillow for \c scripts/generate_placeholders.py.
    \endlist

    \section1 The game

    Open \c CMakeLists.txt in the Qt Creator from the Felgo SDK, select the Felgo desktop kit
    and run the \c appCreatureWars target. The first configure step downloads nlohmann/json,
    and GoogleTest when tests are enabled, so it needs a network connection.

    Debug builds open a second console window called \e {Creature Wars Debug}. It shows log
    messages and a line with the game tick time, the creature count and the dispatcher queue,
    which helps a lot when you look at performance.

    \section1 CMake options

    \table
        \header \li Option \li Default \li Effect
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_BUILD_APP \li \c ON
             \li Build the Felgo application. Turn it off to build only the core and tests.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_MAP_SIZE \li \c 64x32
             \li Map size preset: \c 40x20, \c 48x24, \c 56x28 or \c 64x32.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS \li \c OFF
             \li Treat compiler warnings as errors, as the CI does.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_ENABLE_CLANG_TIDY \li \c OFF
             \li Run clang-tidy on the core while building.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_ENABLE_CPPCHECK \li \c OFF
             \li Run cppcheck on the core while building.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_USE_CCACHE \li \c ON \li Use ccache when it is found.
        \row \li \c CREATURE_WARS_USE_LLD \li \c ON \li Link with lld when it is found.
    \endtable

    \section1 Core and unit tests

    The core builds without Qt and Felgo. That is also how the CI builds it:

    \badcode
    cmake -S . -B build/core -G Ninja -DCREATURE_WARS_BUILD_APP=OFF \
        -DCREATURE_WARS_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS=ON -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build/core
    ctest --test-dir build/core --output-on-failure
    \endcode

    The tests live in \c tests/ and use GoogleTest. They cover the dispatcher, the
    scheduler, the game clock, base orders, the map, the pathfinder, the AI planner and a
    few full games between AI players. Scheduler based logic is tested with fixed points in
    time, so the tests do not wait for real time to pass.

    \section1 Continuous integration

    \c .github/workflows/ci.yml builds the core with GCC and Clang, runs the tests under
    AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and runs clang-tidy and cppcheck. Warnings
    count as errors there, so build with \c CREATURE_WARS_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS=ON before you
    push.

    \section1 Android

    On the todo list. The Android build is not tested yet, so there are no build steps here.

    \section1 This documentation

    The documentation is written in QDoc and lives in \c docs. With \c qdoc in your \c PATH
    (it comes with the SDK) build the \c docs target, or run it by hand:

    \badcode
    cd docs
    qdoc creature_wars.qdocconf
    \endcode

    The HTML ends up in \c docs/html and is committed, so readers can open
    \c docs/html/index.html without building anything. Rebuild it after every change to the
    docs or to quoted code. Code snippets in the tutorial are quoted straight from
    the source files with \c \\quotefromfile, so they stay in sync with the code as long as
    the quoted lines keep existing.
*/
